"use strict";
window.LC_CHUNKS=window.LC_CHUNKS||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en"]=window.LC_CHUNKS["university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en"]||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en"][4]={"university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en:M4-L1":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L1\">M4-L1 – Events and conditional probability<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M4 – Bachelor: Probability and stochastic models (8 lessons)<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor · Core/Fundamentals<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M2.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Model events, calculate\nconditional probabilities and distinguish independence from mutual\nexclusivity.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>From experiment to model<\/h2>\n<p>A random experiment does not necessarily describe a physically random\nprocess. An unknown state of a device can also be modelled as\nuncertainty. The sample space\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Omega<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ncontains all outcomes envisaged in the model. An event\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>A<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis a set of outcomes. The events form a sigma-algebra: it contains\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Omega<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand is closed under complements and countable unions. A probability\nmeasure satisfies\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P(\\Omega)=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nnon-negativity and countable additivity for pairwise disjoint events. It\nfollows that\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msup><mi>A<\/mi><mi>c<\/mi><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P(A^c)=1-P(A)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><mo>∪<\/mo><mi>B<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>+<\/mo><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>B<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>−<\/mo><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><mo>∩<\/mo><mi>B<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P(A\\cup B)=P(A)+P(B)-P(A\\cap B).<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>In a finite sample space with equally likely outcomes, counting\nsuffices. With unequal probabilities, one cannot simply divide by the\nnumber of outcomes.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Conditioning changes the reference set<\/h2>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-data-science-and-artificial-intelligence-en/media/img/001697_en.svg\"\nalt=\"A grid of 100 sensors: 40 older, of which 10 are faulty; 60 newer, of which 6 are faulty.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">A grid of 100 sensors: 40 older, of which\n10 are faulty; 60 newer, of which 6 are faulty.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Conditional probability in a sensor population.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>In a synthetic population of 100 sensors, 40 belong to an older\nseries\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>B<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">B<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nOf these, 10 report a fault\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>A<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nOf the remaining 60, 6 report a fault. When a device is selected\nuniformly,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>16<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>100<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.16<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P(A)=16/100=0.16<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>B<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.4<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P(B)=0.4<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><mo>∩<\/mo><mi>B<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P(A\\cap B)=0.1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>B<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P(B)&gt;0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><mo>∣<\/mo><mi>B<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><mo>∩<\/mo><mi>B<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>B<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mn>10<\/mn><mn>40<\/mn><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.25<\/mn><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P(A\\mid B)=\\frac{P(A\\cap B)}{P(B)}=\\frac{10}{40}=0.25.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>The denominator now covers only the older series. In the reverse\ndirection,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>B<\/mi><mo>∣<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>10<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>16<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.625<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P(B\\mid A)=10/16=0.625<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe two statements answer different questions. The product rule\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><mo>∩<\/mo><mi>B<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>B<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><mo>∣<\/mo><mi>B<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P(A\\cap B)=P(B)P(A\\mid B)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nmakes this direction explicit. For a disjoint, exhaustive partition\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>B<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">B_i<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwith positive probabilities,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mo>∑<\/mo><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><mo>∣<\/mo><msub><mi>B<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>B<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P(A)=\\sum_i P(A\\mid B_i)P(B_i)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Independence and limitations<\/h2>\n<p>Independence means\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><mo>∩<\/mo><mi>B<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>B<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P(A\\cap B)=P(A)P(B)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIn our example, the product would be\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>0.064<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.064<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nnot\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>0.1<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nAge and fault reporting are therefore dependent in the model. This does\nnot yet establish that age causes the fault. Disjoint events with\npositive probabilities are never independent: their intersection has\nprobability zero. Likewise, a probability of zero in continuous models\ndoes not necessarily mean logical impossibility.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercise and solution<\/h2>\n<p>A randomly selected device belongs to the newer series. What is the\nprobability of a fault? Also calculate\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><mo>∪<\/mo><mi>B<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P(A\\cup B)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<p>Solution:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>6<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>60<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">6/60=0.1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe union includes all 40 older devices and the 6 faulty newer devices,\ngiving\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>46<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>100<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.46<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">46/100=0.46<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nCheck using the union formula:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.16<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>0.4<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>0.1<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.46<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.16+0.4-0.1=0.46<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources and further reading<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://online.stat.psu.edu/statprogram/stat414\">Penn\nState, STAT 414: Introduction to Probability Theory (course\noverview)<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> – <strong>Verification:<\/strong> pending.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/18-05-introduction-to-probability-and-statistics-spring-2022/resources/readings/\">MIT\nOpenCourseWare 18.05, Readings (overview)<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> –\n<strong>Verification:<\/strong> pending.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en:M4-L2":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L2\">M4-L2 – Random variables and discrete distributions<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M4 – Bachelor: Probability and stochastic models (8 lessons)<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor · Core/Fundamentals<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M2; <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M4&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M4-L1<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Distinguish and apply discrete\nrandom variables, probability mass functions and typical count\ndistributions.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>From outcome to number<\/h2>\n<p>A random variable\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>X<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">X<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis a measurable mapping from the sample space to the real numbers. In\nthree transmission attempts, the outcome may be a sequence of successes\nand failures, while\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>X<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">X<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nrecords only the number of failures. Different sequences can produce the\nsame value. For a discrete random variable,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p<\/mi><mi>X<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>k<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>X<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>k<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p_X(k)=P(X=k)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p<\/mi><mi>X<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>k<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>≥<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p_X(k)\\geq 0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∑<\/mo><mi>k<\/mi><\/msub><msub><mi>p<\/mi><mi>X<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>k<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sum_k p_X(k)=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe cumulative distribution function\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F<\/mi><mi>X<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>X<\/mi><mo>≤<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F_X(x)=P(X\\leq x)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nsums the masses up to and including\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>x<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\nit is right-continuous and is typically a step function in discrete\nmodels.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Bernoulli and binomial distributions<\/h2>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-data-science-and-artificial-intelligence-en/media/img/001530_en.svg\"\nalt=\"Probability masses for zero to four failures, n = 4 and p = 0.2.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Probability masses for zero to four\nfailures, n = 4 and p = 0.2.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Binomial distribution for four packet trials.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>In a trial with two possible outcomes,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>X<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">X=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nencodes the event of interest and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>X<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">X=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nits absence. The Bernoulli distribution has parameter\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>X<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p=P(X=1)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nSumming\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>n<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nindependent, identically distributed Bernoulli trials produces a\nbinomial variable. For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>k<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mi>…<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k=0,\\dots,n<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>X<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>k<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mfrac linethickness=\"0\"><mi>n<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><msup><mi>p<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mi>p<\/mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mrow><mi>n<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>k<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msup><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P(X=k)=\\binom nk p^k(1-p)^{n-k}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>The binomial coefficient counts the possible arrangements of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>k<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nevents. For four independent packets with failure probability\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p=0.2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe masses for\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>k<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k=0,1,2,3,4<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nare exactly 0.4096, 0.4096, 0.1536, 0.0256 and 0.0016. At least one\nfailure occurs with probability\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>0.4096<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.5904<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1-0.4096=0.5904<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe mass for exactly one failure, by contrast, is 0.4096.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Other discrete models<\/h2>\n<p>The Poisson distribution\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>X<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>k<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mi>e<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mi>λ<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msup><msup><mi>λ<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/msup><mi>/<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><mi>!<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P(X=k)=e^{-\\lambda}\\lambda^k/k!<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwith\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>k<\/mi><mo>≥<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k\\geq0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ<\/mi><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda&gt;0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nmodels, for example, counts in a fixed time window at constant intensity\nand with independent increments of a Poisson process. It approximates a\nbinomial distribution for large\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>n<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nsmall\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>p<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n<\/mi><mi>p<\/mi><mo>≈<\/mo><mi>λ<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">np\\approx\\lambda<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe geometric distribution\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>T<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>k<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mi>p<\/mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mrow><mi>k<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><mi>p<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P(T=k)=(1-p)^{k-1}p<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>k<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mi>…<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k=1,2,\\dots<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndescribes the number of independent trials until the first event\n(<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0<\/mn><mo>&lt;<\/mo><mi>p<\/mi><mo>≤<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0&lt;p\\leq1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>).\nThe successful trial is included. Sampling without replacement from a\nfinite population generally requires another model, such as the\nhypergeometric distribution. Dependent packet failures following a\nnetwork outage also violate the binomial assumptions.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercise and solution<\/h2>\n<p>For four packets, calculate the probability of at most one failure\nand of at least two failures. Which assumption would need to be\nreconsidered if there were a common source of disruption?<\/p>\n<p>Solution:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>X<\/mi><mo>≤<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.8192<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P(X\\leq1)=0.8192<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>X<\/mi><mo>≥<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.1808<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P(X\\geq2)=0.1808<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nCommon disruptions may violate independence; an unchanged\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>p<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nalone is not sufficient.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources and further reading<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://online.stat.psu.edu/statprogram/stat414\">Penn\nState, STAT 414: Introduction to Probability Theory (course\noverview)<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> – <strong>Verification:<\/strong> pending.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/18-05-introduction-to-probability-and-statistics-spring-2022/resources/readings/\">MIT\nOpenCourseWare 18.05, Readings (overview)<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> –\n<strong>Verification:<\/strong> pending.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en:M4-L3":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L3\">M4-L3 – Continuous distributions and densities<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M4 – Bachelor: Probability and stochastic models (8 lessons)<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor · Core/Fundamentals<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M2; <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M4&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M4-L2<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Interpret continuous models,\nintegrate interval probabilities and explain the parameters of common\ndensities.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Probabilities as areas<\/h2>\n<p>For an absolutely continuous random variable\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>X<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">X<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwith density\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>f<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe probability of an interval is<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>a<\/mi><mo>≤<\/mo><mi>X<\/mi><mo>≤<\/mo><mi>b<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msubsup><mo>∫<\/mo><mi>a<\/mi><mi>b<\/mi><\/msubsup><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi>d<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P(a\\leq X\\leq b)=\\int_a^b f(x)\\,dx.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>The density is non-negative and its integral over the real line is\none. A single point has probability zero. The density itself is not a\nprobability: it can exceed one and, for a quantity measured in seconds,\nhas units of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1/\\mathrm{s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe area is dimensionless. From\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>F<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msubsup><mo>∫<\/mo><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mi>∞<\/mi><\/mrow><mi>x<\/mi><\/msubsup><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi>d<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F(x)=\\int_{-\\infty}^x f(t)\\,dt<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nif\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>F<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis differentiable, we recover\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mi>F<\/mi><mo>′<\/mo><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f(x)=F&#39;(x)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThis reversal is not possible for every arbitrary distribution; mixtures\nof point masses and densities also exist.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Uniform distribution and interval calculations<\/h2>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-data-science-and-artificial-intelligence-en/media/img/001585_en.svg\"\nalt=\"Uniform distribution from 0 to 4 seconds; the area between 1 and 3 seconds equals 0.5.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Uniform distribution from 0 to 4 seconds;\nthe area between 1 and 3 seconds equals 0.5.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Probability as integrated density.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>Suppose the waiting time of an idealised inspection cycle is\nuniformly distributed between 0 and 4 seconds. Then\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>4<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f(x)=1/4\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-1}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\non this interval and is zero elsewhere. For 1 to 3 seconds, the area is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>4<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.5<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2\\cdot1/4=0.5<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nOn the interval,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>F<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mn>4<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F(x)=x/4<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwhen time is expressed numerically in seconds; before it the value is\nzero, and after it one. If the same time is expressed in milliseconds,\nthe density becomes 0.00025 per millisecond. The probability remains the\nsame.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Normal and exponential distributions<\/h2>\n<p>The normal distribution\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"script\">𝒩<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>μ<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><msup><mi>σ<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathcal N(\\mu,\\sigma^2)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwith\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ<\/mi><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sigma&gt;0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nhas density<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mrow><mi>σ<\/mi><msqrt><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mi>π<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msqrt><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">exp<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>μ<\/mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><msup><mi>σ<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f(x)=\\frac{1}{\\sigma\\sqrt{2\\pi}}\\exp\\left(-\\frac{(x-\\mu)^2}{2\\sigma^2}\\right).<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>It models, for example, symmetric measurement deviations without hard\nbounds; it is standardised by\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Z<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>X<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>μ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><mi>σ<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Z=(X-\\mu)/\\sigma<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nA different model is often appropriate for positive waiting times. For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo>≥<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x\\geq0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe exponential distribution has density\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ<\/mi><msup><mi>e<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mi>λ<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda e^{-\\lambda x}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand survival function\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>X<\/mi><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mi>e<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mi>λ<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P(X&gt;x)=e^{-\\lambda x}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwith rate\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ<\/mi><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda&gt;0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIt has a constant hazard rate and mean waiting time\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mi>λ<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1/\\lambda<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nNot every waiting time satisfies this assumption; ageing or time limits\nmay contradict it.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercise and solution<\/h2>\n<p>A measurement is uniformly distributed between 2 and 2.4 volts.\nCalculate the density and the probability of 2.1 to 2.2 volts. Explain\nwhy the density may exceed one.<\/p>\n<p>Solution:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>0.4<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2.5<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1/0.4=2.5\\,\\mathrm V^{-1}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\nthe interval width is 0.1 V, so\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.25<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P=0.25<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe density value is probability mass per unit, not the probability of\nan individual point.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources and further reading<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/18-05-introduction-to-probability-and-statistics-spring-2022/resources/readings/\">MIT\nOpenCourseWare 18.05, Readings (overview)<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> –\n<strong>Verification:<\/strong> pending.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://online.stat.psu.edu/stat414/Lesson14\">Penn State,\nSTAT 414, Lesson 14: Continuous Random Variables<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> –\n<strong>Verification:<\/strong> pending.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en:M4-L4":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L4\">M4-L4 – Expectation, variance and covariance<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M4 – Bachelor: Probability and stochastic models (8 lessons)<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor · Core/Fundamentals<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M2; <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M4&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M4-L3<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Calculate expectation, variance\nand covariance and understand their significance for combined\ndecisions.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>An expected value is not always a possible outcome<\/h2>\n<p>For a discrete random variable with finite absolute expectation,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>E<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>X<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mo>∑<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><\/msub><mi>x<\/mi><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>X<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E[X]=\\sum_x xP(X=x)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\nin the absolutely continuous case,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>E<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>X<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mo>∫<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><msub><mi>f<\/mi><mi>X<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi>d<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E[X]=\\int xf_X(x)\\,dx<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nDepending on its load, a system takes 2 or 8 seconds with probabilities\n0.75 and 0.25. The mean time is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>μ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>0.75<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>8<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>0.25<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3.5<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mu=2\\cdot0.75+8\\cdot0.25=3.5<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nseconds. No individual run need take exactly 3.5 seconds. In repeated\nindependent, identically distributed runs, the sample mean approaches\nthis expectation under suitable conditions.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Variance and scaling<\/h2>\n<p>For a finite second moment,<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Var<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>X<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>E<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>X<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>μ<\/mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>E<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><msup><mi>X<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mo>−<\/mo><msup><mi>μ<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\operatorname{Var}(X)=E[(X-\\mu)^2]=E[X^2]-\\mu^2.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>In the load example,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>E<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><msup><mi>X<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.75<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>0.25<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>64<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>19<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E[X^2]=0.75\\cdot4+0.25\\cdot64=19<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nhence\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Var<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>X<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>6.75<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\operatorname{Var}(X)=6.75\\,\\mathrm{s}^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ<\/mi><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>2.598<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sigma\\approx2.598\\,\\mathrm s<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe standard deviation has the same unit as the measured quantity. For\nconstants\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>b<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a,b<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>E<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>a<\/mi><mi>X<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi>b<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>a<\/mi><mi>E<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>X<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mo>+<\/mo><mi>b<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E[aX+b]=aE[X]+b<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Var<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>a<\/mi><mi>X<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi>b<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mi>a<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Var<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>X<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\operatorname{Var}(aX+b)=a^2\\operatorname{Var}(X)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFor heavy-tailed models, the existence of moments must be checked; the\nCauchy distribution, for example, has no defined ordinary\nexpectation.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Two quantities and covariance<\/h2>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-data-science-and-artificial-intelligence-en/media/img/001615_en.svg\"\nalt=\"Two equally likely pairs (1,2) and (3,6) lie below left and above right of the mean (2,4).\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Two equally likely pairs (1,2) and (3,6)\nlie below left and above right of the mean (2,4).<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Covariance as joint deviation.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>The covariance\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Cov<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>X<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>Y<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>E<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>X<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>E<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>X<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>Y<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>E<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>Y<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\operatorname{Cov}(X,Y)=E[(X-E[X])(Y-E[Y])]<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ncaptures joint linear deviations. In a model with equally likely pairs\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>X<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>Y<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>,<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>6<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(X,Y)=(1,2),(3,6)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe means are 2 and 4, the variances 1 and 4, and the covariance 2. The\nPearson correlation\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Cov<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>X<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>Y<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>σ<\/mi><mi>X<\/mi><\/msub><msub><mi>σ<\/mi><mi>Y<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\rho=\\operatorname{Cov}(X,Y)/(\\sigma_X\\sigma_Y)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis 1 here. It is defined only when both standard deviations are\npositive. With finite second moments, independence implies zero\ncovariance, but not conversely. For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>X<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">X<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nuniformly distributed over\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-1,0,1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mi>X<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Y=X^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndepends on\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>X<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">X<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nyet\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>E<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>X<\/mi><mi>Y<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mi>E<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>X<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mi>E<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>Y<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E[XY]=0=E[X]E[Y]<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercise and solution<\/h2>\n<p>For the two quantities in the paired example, calculate\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>E<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>X<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi>Y<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E[X+Y]<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Var<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>X<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi>Y<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\operatorname{Var}(X+Y)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<p>Solution: expectations add without an independence assumption: 6. The\nvariance is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>9<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1+4+2\\cdot2=9<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe two possible sums, 3 and 9, are each three units from the mean.\nOmitting the covariance underestimates the spread in this model.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources and further reading<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://online.stat.psu.edu/statprogram/stat414\">Penn\nState, STAT 414: Introduction to Probability Theory (course\noverview)<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> – <strong>Verification:<\/strong> pending.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/18-05-introduction-to-probability-and-statistics-spring-2022/resources/readings/\">MIT\nOpenCourseWare 18.05, Readings (overview)<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> –\n<strong>Verification:<\/strong> pending.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en:M4-L5":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L5\">M4-L5 – Joint distributions and conditional independence<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M4 – Bachelor: Probability and stochastic models (8 lessons)<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor · Core/Fundamentals<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M2; <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M4&amp;lesson=L4&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M4-L4<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Distinguish joint, marginal and\nconditional distributions and check conditional independence in a\nmodel.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Joint probabilities<\/h2>\n<p>A joint discrete distribution contains\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>y<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>X<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>Y<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>y<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p(x,y)=P(X=x,Y=y)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand sums to one over all pairs. The marginal distribution is obtained by\nsumming over the other variable:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p<\/mi><mi>X<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mo>∑<\/mo><mi>y<\/mi><\/msub><mi>p<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>y<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p_X(x)=\\sum_y p(x,y)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p<\/mi><mi>X<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p_X(x)&gt;0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>y<\/mi><mo>∣<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>p<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>y<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><msub><mi>p<\/mi><mi>X<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p(y\\mid x)=p(x,y)/p_X(x)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nWith a joint density, integration over the other variable replaces\nsummation; the conditional density is defined as a quotient for suitable\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>x<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwith positive marginal density. The factorisation\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>y<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>p<\/mi><mi>X<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><msub><mi>p<\/mi><mi>Y<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>y<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p(x,y)=p_X(x)p_Y(y)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor all pairs characterises discrete independence.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>A common influence<\/h2>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-data-science-and-artificial-intelligence-en/media/img/001507_en.svg\"\nalt=\"Averaging two conditional tables with independent alarms yields a dependent joint distribution.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Averaging two conditional tables with\nindependent alarms yields a dependent joint distribution.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Conditional and marginal independence.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>Two alarms\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>B<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A,B<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\neach trigger with probability 0.8 in state\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Z<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Z=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\n(high load) and with probability 0.2 in state\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Z<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Z=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nBoth states are equally likely. Assume the alarms are independent within\neach state. Then<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>B<\/mi><mo>∣<\/mo><mi>Z<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>z<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><mo>∣<\/mo><mi>Z<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>z<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>B<\/mi><mo>∣<\/mo><mi>Z<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>z<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P(A,B\\mid Z=z)=P(A\\mid Z=z)P(B\\mid Z=z).<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>Here\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>A<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>B<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">B<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\neach denote a triggered alarm. Without knowledge of the state,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>B<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.5<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P(A)=P(B)=0.5<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwhereas\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><mo>∩<\/mo><mi>B<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.5<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>0.64<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>0.5<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>0.04<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.34<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P(A\\cap B)=0.5\\cdot0.64+0.5\\cdot0.04=0.34<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe product of the marginal probabilities is 0.25. The alarms are\ntherefore marginally dependent, even though they are independent within\neach state group.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Benefits and limitations of factorisation<\/h2>\n<p>The joint distribution of all three variables can be written as\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>z<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>p<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>a<\/mi><mo>∣<\/mo><mi>z<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>p<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>b<\/mi><mo>∣<\/mo><mi>z<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p(z)p(a\\mid z)p(b\\mid z)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin this case. Thus, for the two binary alarms, only the individual\nprobabilities for each state need to be known, rather than parametrising\nall alarm combinations independently. Such assumptions are a core\ncomponent of probabilistic models. They must, however, be justified in\nthe application domain: two alarms connected to the same faulty\ncontroller may be dependent even if the load state is known. Additional\nconditioning can also create dependencies; when selection is influenced\nby both variables, initially independent quantities can become dependent\nwithin the selected group.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercise and solution<\/h2>\n<p>Determine the joint table of alarm outcomes without knowing\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>Z<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Z<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand the probability that B triggers given that A has triggered.<\/p>\n<p>Solution:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.34<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P(1,1)=0.34<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.16<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P(1,0)=0.16<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.16<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P(0,1)=0.16<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.34<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P(0,0)=0.34<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe sum is one. Conditional on A, the probability is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.34<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>0.5<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.68<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.34/0.5=0.68<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nWithout knowledge of A, it was 0.5.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources and further reading<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://online.stat.psu.edu/statprogram/stat414\">Penn\nState, STAT 414: Introduction to Probability Theory (course\noverview)<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> – <strong>Verification:<\/strong> pending.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://online.stat.psu.edu/stat414/Lesson20\">Penn State,\nSTAT 414, Lesson 20: Distributions of Two Continuous Random\nVariables<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> – <strong>Verification:<\/strong> pending.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en:M4-L6":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L6\">M4-L6 – Bayes' theorem and probabilistic prediction<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M4 – Bachelor: Probability and stochastic models (8 lessons)<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor · Core/Fundamentals<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M2; <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M4&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M4-L5<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Distinguish the prior,\nlikelihood, posterior and predictive probabilities, and calculate with\nbase rates.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Information updates a model<\/h2>\n<p>For two events\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>H<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>D<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">D<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwith\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>D<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P(D)&gt;0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>H<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P(H)&gt;0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe product rule gives<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>H<\/mi><mo>∣<\/mo><mi>D<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>D<\/mi><mo>∣<\/mo><mi>H<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>H<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>D<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P(H\\mid D)=\\frac{P(D\\mid H)P(H)}{P(D)}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>H<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P(H)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis called the prior probability;\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>D<\/mi><mo>∣<\/mo><mi>H<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P(D\\mid H)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nviewed as a function of the hypothesis, is called the likelihood. The\ndenominator is the evidence; it normalises the posterior probabilities.\nThe likelihood is not automatically a distribution over hypotheses. For\nan exhaustive disjoint set of discrete hypotheses,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>D<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mo>∑<\/mo><mi>h<\/mi><\/msub><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>D<\/mi><mo>∣<\/mo><mi>h<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>h<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P(D)=\\sum_h P(D\\mid h)P(h)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Inspection signal and base rate<\/h2>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-data-science-and-artificial-intelligence-en/media/img/001520_en.svg\"\nalt=\"Among 10,000 components, 180 true and 490 false alarms are expected; 180 of 670 alarms represent real defects.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Among 10,000 components, 180 true and 490\nfalse alarms are expected; 180 of 670 alarms represent real\ndefects.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Base rate and informativeness of an alarm.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>A synthetic quality inspector raises an alarm for 90% of genuinely\ndefective components. An alarm is also raised for 5% of fault-free\ncomponents. The defect rate is 2%. Among 10,000 representative\ncomponents, the model probabilities correspond to expected counts of 200\ndefective and 9,800 fault-free components. We expect 180 true alarms and\n490 false alarms. Given an alarm, therefore,<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>H<\/mi><mo>∣<\/mo><mi>D<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>0.02<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>0.9<\/mn><\/mrow><mrow><mn>0.02<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>0.9<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>0.98<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>0.05<\/mn><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mn>180<\/mn><mn>670<\/mn><\/mfrac><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>0.269<\/mn><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P(H\\mid D)=\\frac{0.02\\cdot0.9}{0.02\\cdot0.9+0.98\\cdot0.05}=\\frac{180}{670}\\approx0.269.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>A high detection rate is thus not equivalent to a high probability of\nan actual defect after the signal. The result depends on the base rate\nand the false alarm rate.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>From hypothesis to prediction<\/h2>\n<p>A posterior distribution over an unknown model parameter can be used\nfor a new observation. In the discrete case,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>Y<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">u<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>y<\/mi><mo>∣<\/mo><mi>D<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mo>∑<\/mo><mi>h<\/mi><\/msub><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>Y<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">u<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>y<\/mi><mo>∣<\/mo><mi>h<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>D<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>h<\/mi><mo>∣<\/mo><mi>D<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P(Y_{\\mathrm{neu}}=y\\mid D)=\\sum_h P(Y_{\\mathrm{neu}}=y\\mid h,D)P(h\\mid D)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIf the new observation is independent of the previous data given\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>h<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe first factor simplifies to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>Y<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">u<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>y<\/mi><mo>∣<\/mo><mi>h<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P(Y_{\\mathrm{neu}}=y\\mid h)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nA second independent inspection of the same component would again have\nalarm rates of 0.9 and 0.05 conditional on the known defect status.\nAfter the first alarm, the predicted probability of a second alarm is\ntherefore\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.9<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>180<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>670<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>0.05<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>490<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>670<\/mn><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>0.2784<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.9\\cdot180/670+0.05\\cdot490/670\\approx0.2784<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nRepetitions of the same measurement error would not constitute\nindependent evidence.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercise and solution<\/h2>\n<p>Repeat the calculation from S2 with a defect base rate of 10%. What\nhas changed?<\/p>\n<p>Solution:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.09<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0.09<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>0.045<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>0.667<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.09/(0.09+0.045)=2/3\\approx0.667<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe inspection rates remain the same, but the higher prior changes the\nposterior. A classification decision additionally requires the costs or\nbenefits of the possible decisions.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources and further reading<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://online.stat.psu.edu/statprogram/stat414\">Penn\nState, STAT 414: Introduction to Probability Theory (course\noverview)<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> – <strong>Verification:<\/strong> pending.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/18-05-introduction-to-probability-and-statistics-spring-2022/resources/readings/\">MIT\nOpenCourseWare 18.05, Readings (overview)<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> –\n<strong>Verification:<\/strong> pending.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en:M4-L7":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L7\">M4-L7 – Law of large numbers and central limit theorem<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M4 – Bachelor: Probability and stochastic models (8 lessons)<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor · Core/Fundamentals<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M2; <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M4&amp;lesson=L6&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M4-L6<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Distinguish the consistency of\nthe sample mean from asymptotic normality and calculate typical\nestimation variability.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Mean as the sample grows<\/h2>\n<p>Let\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>,<\/mo><mi>…<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><msub><mi>X<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">X_1,\\ldots,X_n<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nbe independent and identically distributed with mean\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>μ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mu<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand finite variance\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mi>σ<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sigma^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe sample mean\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover><mi>X<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾<\/mo><\/mover><mi>n<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mi>n<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><msub><mo>∑<\/mo><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><msub><mi>X<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\bar X_n=n^{-1}\\sum_i X_i<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nsatisfies\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>E<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><msub><mover><mi>X<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾<\/mo><\/mover><mi>n<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>μ<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E[\\bar X_n]=\\mu<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Var<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mover><mi>X<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾<\/mo><\/mover><mi>n<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mi>σ<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mi>/<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\operatorname{Var}(\\bar X_n)=\\sigma^2/n<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nChebyshev’s inequality gives, for every\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ε<\/mi><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\varepsilon&gt;0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><msub><mover><mi>X<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾<\/mo><\/mover><mi>n<\/mi><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><mi>μ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mo>≥<\/mo><mi>ε<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>≤<\/mo><mfrac><msup><mi>σ<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mrow><mi>n<\/mi><msup><mi>ε<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo>→<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P(|\\bar X_n-\\mu|\\geq\\varepsilon)\\leq\\frac{\\sigma^2}{n\\varepsilon^2}\\longrightarrow0.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>Under these assumptions, this proves the weak law of large numbers:\nconvergence in probability. It does not say that every new observation\nreduces the error. Under suitable assumptions, the strong law gives\nalmost sure convergence; these two concepts must be distinguished.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Central limit theorem<\/h2>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-data-science-and-artificial-intelligence-en/media/img/001591_en.svg\"\nalt=\"Three normal approximations for Bernoulli means with p=0.3 and n=25, 100, 400 show decreasing standard errors.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Three normal approximations for Bernoulli\nmeans with p=0.3 and n=25, 100, 400 show decreasing standard\nerrors.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Spread of the sample mean.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>Under the preceding assumptions with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0<\/mn><mo>&lt;<\/mo><msup><mi>σ<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>&lt;<\/mo><mi>∞<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0&lt;\\sigma^2&lt;\\infty<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mrow><msqrt><mi>n<\/mi><\/msqrt><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mover><mi>X<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾<\/mo><\/mover><mi>n<\/mi><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><mi>μ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>σ<\/mi><\/mfrac><mover><mo>→<\/mo><mi>d<\/mi><\/mover><mi mathvariant=\"script\">𝒩<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\frac{\\sqrt n(\\bar X_n-\\mu)}{\\sigma}\\xrightarrow{d}\\mathcal N(0,1).<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>The standardised deviation converges in distribution, not to a single\nfixed value. The theorem explains why many sample means are\napproximately normally distributed even though individual values are not\nnormally distributed. For Bernoulli variables with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.3<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p=0.3<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>σ<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.21<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sigma^2=0.21<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>25<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>100<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>400<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n=25,100,400<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe standard errors are approximately 0.0917, 0.0458 and 0.0229. The\ndistribution of individual observations remains unchanged; only the\nmeans become increasingly concentrated.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>What the central limit theorem does not guarantee<\/h2>\n<p>There is no magic sample size valid for all distributions at which\nthe normal approximation becomes accurate. Very rare events, strongly\nskewed distributions and heavy tails can impair the approximation. For\nobservations correlated over time, the variance formula generally does\nnot hold: covariances between observations must be included. More data\nfrom a biased selection process may cause the sample mean to converge\nprecisely to the wrong target. Increasing the sample size therefore\nreplaces neither sound data collection nor model validation.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercise and solution<\/h2>\n<p>Suppose an independent measurement process has\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>6<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sigma=6<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nms. How many measurements are needed for a standard error of at most 0.5\nms? What is the Chebyshev bound for a deviation of at least 2 ms at this\nsample size?<\/p>\n<p>Solution:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>6<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><msqrt><mi>n<\/mi><\/msqrt><mo>≤<\/mo><mn>0.5<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">6/\\sqrt n\\leq0.5<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nrequires\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n<\/mi><mo>≥<\/mo><mn>144<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n\\geq144<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe bound is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>36<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>144<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.0625<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">36/(144\\cdot4)=0.0625<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIt is an upper bound, not the exact error probability.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources and further reading<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/18-05-introduction-to-probability-and-statistics-spring-2022/resources/readings/\">MIT\nOpenCourseWare 18.05, Readings (overview)<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> –\n<strong>Verification:<\/strong> pending.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://online.stat.psu.edu/stat414/Section05\">Penn State,\nSTAT 414, Section 5: Distributions of Functions of Random Variables<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a>\n– <strong>Verification:<\/strong> pending.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en:M4-L8":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L8\">M4-L8 – Simulation and Monte Carlo fundamentals<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M4 – Bachelor: Probability and stochastic models (8 lessons)<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor · Core/Fundamentals<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M2; <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M4&amp;lesson=L7&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M4-L7<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Construct Monte Carlo\nestimators, quantify simulation error and implement essential conditions\nfor reproducibility.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Simulation as expectation calculation<\/h2>\n<p>Monte Carlo methods approximate quantities using randomly generated\nsamples. For independent draws\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>X<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">X_i<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfrom a specified model and a function\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>g<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover><mi>θ<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂<\/mo><\/mover><mi>n<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mi>n<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><msub><mo>∑<\/mo><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mi>g<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>X<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\hat\\theta_n=n^{-1}\\sum_i g(X_i)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis an estimator of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>θ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>E<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>g<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>X<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\theta=E[g(X)]<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nWith a finite second moment, its variance is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Var<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>g<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>X<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\operatorname{Var}(g(X))/n<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nAn event probability is obtained with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>g<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>X<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mn mathvariant=\"bold\">𝟏<\/mn><mi>A<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>X<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g(X)=\\mathbf1_A(X)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\nthe mean of the zeros and ones is the observed hit rate. The standard\ndeviation of the estimator can be estimated by\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s<\/mi><mi>g<\/mi><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><msqrt><mi>n<\/mi><\/msqrt><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s_g/\\sqrt n<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nModel inaccuracy must be assessed separately from this numerical\nsampling error.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Evaluating an integral numerically<\/h2>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-data-science-and-artificial-intelligence-en/media/img/001650_en.svg\"\nalt=\"The theoretical standard error of the integral estimator for U² decreases in proportion to the inverse square root of sample size.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">The theoretical standard error of the\nintegral estimator for U² decreases in proportion to the inverse square\nroot of sample size.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Monte Carlo error and sample size.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>Suppose we want to evaluate the integral\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>I<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msubsup><mo>∫<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mn>1<\/mn><\/msubsup><msup><mi>x<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi>d<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I=\\int_0^1 x^2\\,dx=1/3<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nSince the uniform distribution on\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">[0,1]<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nhas density one,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>I<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>E<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><msup><mi>U<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I=E[U^2]<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe analytical variance of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mi>U<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>5<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>9<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>45<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1/5-1/9=4/45<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFor 10,000 independent draws, the theoretical standard error is about\n0.002981.<\/p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb1\"><pre\nclass=\"sourceCode python\"><code class=\"sourceCode python\"><span id=\"cb1-1\"><a href=\"#cb1-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"im\">import<\/span> numpy <span class=\"im\">as<\/span> np<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-2\"><a href=\"#cb1-2\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>rng <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> np.random.default_rng(<span class=\"dv\">731<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-3\"><a href=\"#cb1-3\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>u <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> rng.random(<span class=\"dv\">10_000<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-4\"><a href=\"#cb1-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>g <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> u<span class=\"op\">**<\/span><span class=\"dv\">2<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-5\"><a href=\"#cb1-5\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>estimate <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> g.mean()<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-6\"><a href=\"#cb1-6\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>se <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> g.std(ddof<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>) <span class=\"op\">/<\/span> np.sqrt(g.size)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-7\"><a href=\"#cb1-7\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"bu\">print<\/span>(<span class=\"ss\">f&quot;I = <\/span><span class=\"sc\">{<\/span>estimate<span class=\"sc\">:.6f}<\/span><span class=\"ss\">, SE = <\/span><span class=\"sc\">{<\/span>se<span class=\"sc\">:.6f}<\/span><span class=\"ss\">&quot;<\/span>)<\/span><\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<p>The seed allows reproduction in the same suitable environment and\nwith the same sequence of generator calls. NumPy does not guarantee\nidentical bit streams across versions for <code>Generator<\/code>. The\nresult may differ slightly from 1/3; a result close to the target alone\ndoes not prove that the implementation is correct. Given sufficiently\nmany independent draws, an approximate 95% interval for the modelled\nexpectation is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover><mi>θ<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂<\/mo><\/mover><mi>n<\/mi><\/msub><mo>±<\/mo><mn>1.96<\/mn><mover><mrow><mi>S<\/mi><mi>E<\/mi><\/mrow><mo accent=\"true\">̂<\/mo><\/mover><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\hat\\theta_n\\pm1.96\\widehat{SE}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIt describes the repeated-sampling coverage of the procedure, not the\nquality of the underlying model.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Efficiency and risks<\/h2>\n<p>The typical error size decreases only as\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><msqrt><mi>n<\/mi><\/msqrt><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1/\\sqrt n<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nA tenfold reduction in standard deviation requires a hundred times as\nmany draws. For extremely rare events, a naive simulation can produce\nmostly zeros and misleadingly yield an empirical spread of zero.\nAlternatives such as importance sampling alter the sampling distribution\nand correct for this by weighting; they require additional mathematical\nconditions. The naive standard error is likewise not applicable to\ndependent simulations such as Markov chains. Document the random source,\nsample size, model assumptions and diagnostics.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercise and solution<\/h2>\n<p>How many draws does the integral example require for a theoretical\nstandard error of at most 0.001?<\/p>\n<p>Solution:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>4<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>45<\/mn><mi>n<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><\/msqrt><mo>≤<\/mo><mn>0.001<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sqrt{4/(45n)}\\leq0.001<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ngives\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n<\/mi><mo>≥<\/mo><mn>88<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>888.89<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n\\geq88{,}888.89<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nRounding up, 88,889 draws are required. This statement does not apply to\na guaranteed maximum absolute error.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources and further reading<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/18-05-introduction-to-probability-and-statistics-spring-2022/resources/readings/\">MIT\nOpenCourseWare 18.05, Readings (overview)<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> –\n<strong>Verification:<\/strong> pending.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://numpy.org/doc/stable/reference/random/generator.html\">NumPy,\nRandom Generator, API reference<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> – <strong>Verification:<\/strong>\npending.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>"};
